menu "OpenAudioLink Test Node"

    config OAL_WIFI_SSID
        string "Wi-Fi SSID"
        default ""
        help
            SSID of the network to join. Leave empty to boot without
            connecting (useful for build tests). Runtime USB provisioning
            replaces this setting in Phase 2.5.

    config OAL_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "Wi-Fi password"
        default ""

    config OAL_NODE_NAME
        string "Node name"
        default "testnode"
        help
            Human-readable name announced during discovery.

    config OAL_I2S_ENABLED
        bool "Play received audio through an I2S DAC"
        default y
        help
            Consumers bring up an I2S output and play what they receive.
            Turn it off for a node with no DAC attached: the RTP statistics
            are unaffected, and every link measurement so far was made
            without one.

    config OAL_I2S_BCLK_GPIO
        int "I2S bit clock (BCK) GPIO"
        depends on OAL_I2S_ENABLED
        default 7
        help
            D8 on a XIAO ESP32S3. The three defaults are chosen so that a
            PCM5102A laid beside the board wires with no crossed leads —
            see docs/HARDWARE.md. The ESP32-S3 routes I2S through its GPIO
            matrix so any free pin works, but avoid D6 and D7, which are
            the UART the serial log comes out of.

    config OAL_I2S_WS_GPIO
        int "I2S word select (LRCK) GPIO"
        depends on OAL_I2S_ENABLED
        default 9

    config OAL_I2S_DOUT_GPIO
        int "I2S data (DIN) GPIO"
        depends on OAL_I2S_ENABLED
        default 8

    config OAL_PLAYOUT_MS
        int "Playout buffer (ms)"
        depends on OAL_I2S_ENABLED
        default 100
        range 5 100
        help
            How much audio to gather before playing any of it, which is
            also how far the speaker lags the source. The DMA adds another
            20 ms on top.

            Size this against the sender's cadence, not the network's
            jitter. Wi-Fi jitter is 1-2 ms, but a Windows sender wakes on a
            15.6 ms system timer and emits packets in clumps, so a 20 ms
            buffer spent most of itself waiting out one gap and ran dry
            several times a second on the first hardware test.

            Then size it against the worst gap rather than the average
            one: with the sender fixed the ring still met occasional
            60-70 ms holes, which a 60 ms target could not survive.

    config OAL_ADC_ENABLED
        bool "Capture line input through an I2S ADC"
        default n
        help
            Producers bring up an I2S input and send what they capture.
            Off by default: a Producer with no ADC still streams its
            synthetic sources, which is how every link measurement so far
            was made.

            Which end owns the clock is the board's decision, not a
            preference — see OAL_ADC_SLAVE below and docs/HARDWARE.md.

    config OAL_ADC_BCLK_GPIO
        int "ADC bit clock (BCK) GPIO"
        depends on OAL_ADC_ENABLED
        default 4
        help
            GPIO 3, 4, 5 and 6 are D2, D3, D4 and D5 on a XIAO ESP32S3 —
            four adjacent pins on the side opposite the DAC's D8/D9/D10,
            so a node can carry both boards without either reaching across.

            Separate pins from the DAC's on purpose. A node may hold both
            roles — capture a turntable and play a stream — and the
            ESP32-S3 has two I2S peripherals, so sharing pins would rule
            that out for no gain.

            Note these are GPIO numbers, not the D labels: the pins to
            avoid are D6 and D7, which are GPIO43 and GPIO44 and carry the
            serial log.

    config OAL_ADC_WS_GPIO
        int "ADC word select (LRCK) GPIO"
        depends on OAL_ADC_ENABLED
        default 5

    config OAL_ADC_DIN_GPIO
        int "ADC data in (DOUT on the module) GPIO"
        depends on OAL_ADC_ENABLED
        default 6

    config OAL_ADC_SLAVE
        bool "The ADC generates the clocks and the ESP32 follows"
        depends on OAL_ADC_ENABLED
        default y
        help
            On by default, because the common "ANA TO I2S" modules carry
            their own 24.576 MHz oscillator: the PCM1808 runs from it and
            generates BCK and LRCK, and the header exposes MCLK as an
            output. Their sample rate is set by that crystal and the
            module's strapping — 48 kHz at 512fs, 96 kHz at 256fs — and
            the node's capture trace measures which you actually got.

            Turn it off for a bare PCM1808, which has no oscillator: then
            the ESP32 drives MCLK, BCK and LRCK and the module follows.
            That is the better arrangement where the board allows it,
            because it puts capture and playback on one clock.

            Getting this wrong is not subtle. Two masters driving one
            clock line produce nothing usable, and the symptom is silence.

    config OAL_ADC_MCLK_GPIO
        int "ADC master clock (SCKI) GPIO"
        depends on OAL_ADC_ENABLED
        default 3
        help
            Used only when this end is master, and harmless otherwise: the
            firmware leaves the pin unassigned in slave mode. Kept visible
            rather than hidden behind !OAL_ADC_SLAVE so the symbol always
            exists — a setting that disappears is a compile error waiting
            for whoever changes the other one. A bare PCM1808 needs a system
            clock and produces nothing at all without it — a silence that
            looks exactly like bad wiring everywhere else. Leave the pin
            unwired against a self-clocked module: its MCLK is an output,
            and two drivers on one line is how boards get damaged.

endmenu
